{
  "id": "Compound::DB00672 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpropamide, a sulfonylurea compound, is strongly associated with the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its mechanism of action as an antidiabetic agent. The evidence from the visible paths indicates that Chlorpropamide is included in the class of sulfonylurea compounds, which are known to inhibit pancreatic beta-cell function by stimulating insulin secretion. This mechanism is supported by the fact that multiple sulfonylureas, such as Tolbutamide, Glipizide, and Glyburide, are all classified under the same antidiabetic agent category and are linked to the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. The shared lowest common ancestors in the paths—Sulfonylurea Antidiabetic Agent and N-Sulfonylurea—further reinforce the biological relevance of this class of compounds in the context of diabetes management. The consistent scores across the paths suggest a robust and well-documented relationship, with the core mechanism being the inhibition of insulin secretion through sulfonylurea action. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Chlorpropamide and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, highlighting the role of sulfonylurea compounds in antidiabetic therapy.",
